-## 5. Paper Organization
+## 4. Contributions and hypotheses
 
-The remainder of this paper is structured as follows:
-* **Section 2** formalizes our macro- and microscopic evaluation metrics and details the demographic validation of our synthetic cohort (Milestone 0).
-* **Section 3** evaluates bare LLM performance across multiple models and quantifies stochastic multi-seed variability across 5 fixed random seeds.
-* **Section 4** details the ablation study confronting the bare model with the prompt enriched with a behavioral decision grid and qualitative guidelines, complemented by microscopic (trip-level) evaluation against the supervised LightGBM oracle, materializing the Tier 3 Ceiling.
-* **Section 5** demonstrates the Tier 2 value-add of generative agents through 5-day behavioral hysteresis kinetics and ecological adaptation to 30 local news events.
-* **Section 6** develops the prospective cascading hybrid architecture and discusses metropolitan scalability.
-* Finally, **Section 7** concludes with key takeaways and perspectives for urban mobility modeling.
+The study is conducted on the Toulouse metropolitan area, against the 2023 Cerema-certified household travel survey (EMC² 2023; Tisséo Collectivités & AUAT, 2023), which covers about 16,000 respondents over 453 municipalities. <!-- source: rapport AUAT/CEREMA 2023 ; BIBLIOGRAPHIE.md §5 -->
+
+**C1. An evaluation contract at informational parity.** Our first contribution is methodological, and it is the one we consider most transferable to other evaluations of LLM agents. The contract has four rules. Each closes one way in which a comparison between a generative agent and a tabular model can be biased.
+
+1. *Informational parity.* The LLM agent, the multinomial logit and the supervised oracle receive the same vector of 21 variables, the production contract `spec_version 2`. <!-- source: scripts/progedo_logit/feature_spec.json --> Without this rule, any gap between models is confounded with a gap in information. Parity concerns inputs, not exposure: the oracle has been fitted on 31,279 survey trips <!-- source: scripts/progedo_logit/mode_choice_policy_metrics.json, training.n_fit --> and the LLM has seen none. The oracle is therefore a reference ceiling, not a competitor.
+2. *Like-for-like readings.* The agent does not pick one itinerary. Following the finding of Meister & Guestrin (2024) that verbalised distributions align better than sampled ones, it is asked to spread 100 % over the itineraries presented, up to six per trip. <!-- source: experience.yaml of the played experiments, max_candidats: 6 --> The platform reads this answer twice: as a probability mass per mode, and as a retained decision. Each reading is compared only with its counterpart: mass against the renormalised probabilities of the tabular model, decision against its argmax. A probabilistic model has two L1 errors, and only one of them is comparable to hard decisions.
+3. *Renormalisation on the offer.* The tabular model predicts over four modes blindly; the agent chooses only among the itineraries the router actually returns. Every tabular prediction is restricted to the modes offered for that trip and renormalised to 100 %, an independence-of-irrelevant-alternatives assumption that we declare as a limit. Without this rule, the tabular model is credited for options that do not exist and the agent is penalised for modes it was never offered.
+4. *Mandatory sample size.* Divergence measures are biased upward on small strata. No score is published without its number of decisions, and every comparison between substrates is accompanied by a no-model witness that isolates the effect of the sample alone.
+
+The evaluation substrate is a sealed synthetic cohort of 1,000 personas <!-- source: data/population/population_1000_AAMAS/MANIFEST.yaml, seal 1 of 2026-09-02, sha256 f67b0777… -->, drawn by proportional allocation on residence ring × motorisation from an eqasim pool of 5,063 persons (Hörl & Balać, 2021), and controlled against the published EMC² margins: six margins judged conformant by the control procedure, two not measurable for lack of a published target. <!-- source: data/population/population_1000_AAMAS/CONTROLE.md --> Their activity chains produce 2,693 trips on the evaluated day, of which 2,645 are evaluable. <!-- source: compteurs.json of the executions of 2026-09-07 --> The tabular models are additionally characterised on the sealed survey test set of 13,045 trips, split by household. <!-- source: mode_choice_policy_metrics.json, test.n_rows, split.by = hh_id --> To our knowledge, none of the LLM-mobility evaluations of Section 2.2 confronts an agent population with a certified survey under such a contract.
+
+**C2. An ablation in four levels, and two hypotheses on the distributional ceiling.** We compare, on the same sealed substrate, four levels of increasing information. Level 0: floors and physical heuristics, a uniform draw over the offer, the majority mode, the shortest itinerary. Level 1: the bare LLM, a neutral instruction and no behavioural guidance. Level 2: the same LLM with a calibrated prompt and, in a separate arm, with *k* survey examples in context. Level 3: the tabular references, multinomial logit and LightGBM oracle (Ke et al., 2017). The LLMs evaluated to date are Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite and Mistral Small, both at temperature 0 <!-- source: data/experiences/exp_gemini-31-fl_minper_jtir_t0_nosim, exp_mistral-s_minper_jtir_t0_nosim -->; further models will be added as their runs complete. Two hypotheses are pre-registered.
+
+- **H0, distributional ceiling.** Neither the bare nor the calibrated LLM reaches the tabular reference within the equivalence margin on the four-mode split. Bare LLM L1 error: **[xx.y | exp_01a]** and **[xx.y | exp_01b]**. Calibrated prompt: **[xx.y | exp_02a]**. Few-shot: **[xx.y | exp_02b]**. Logit: **[xx.y | exp_03a]**. Oracle: **[xx.y | exp_03b]**. H0 is refuted if the few-shot arm enters the equivalence margin of the oracle: the ceiling would then be informational, not structural.
+- **H1, primacy of physical impedance.** A change in the terminal times the router attributes to access and parking moves the agents' modal split more than the enrichment of the prompt does. Prompt effect: **[xx.y | exp_02a − exp_01a]**. Impedance effect: **[xx.y | terminal-time arm, to be added to experiments.yaml]**. Both effects are measured on the same personas with a paired test and reported with their inter-seed dispersion.
+
+**C3. Two regimes no survey tabulates, evaluated for robustness rather than fidelity.**
+
+- *Five-day hysteresis after a service breakdown.* On day 2 of a five-day horizon, metro line A fails at 17:00 and imposes a 45-minute delay; the network is nominal again from day 3. <!-- source: experiments.yaml, exp_04a perturbation --> Three arms are compared: an agent with a short-term memory buffer in the sense of Park et al. (2023), decaying at rate λ; the same agent without memory; the tabular oracle, structurally memoryless. No survey follows the same travellers through such an event, so no value is a target. What is testable is the order of the arms on day 3, **[xx.y | exp_04a vs exp_04b vs exp_04c]**, the monotonic recovery over days 3 to 5, and the displacement of the curve when λ is divided by three, **[xx.y | exp_04d]**. The hypothesis falls if the memoryless agent shows the same inertia, if λ has no effect, or if recovery is not monotonic.
